{
  "version": 1,
  "courseId": 1362070,
  "sourceLanguage": "en",
  "targetLanguage": "zh-TW",
  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "38345384",
      "title": "Reference vs Primitive Values",
      "sourceHash": "5bd421155a7863905542f6fcba889b8d3d124b88066f2577a5ce73cf5f092a2c",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 2.04,
          "end": 4.11,
          "en": "Now the last crucial concept",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 4.11,
          "end": 7.89,
          "en": "I wanna cover here is related to reference",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 7.89,
          "end": 10.56,
          "en": "versus primitive values.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 10.56,
          "end": 12.66,
          "en": "And what do I mean by that?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 12.66,
          "end": 17.31,
          "en": "Well, if we got a string like user message here,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 17.31,
          "end": 19.44,
          "en": "that's a primitive value.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 19.44,
          "end": 23.1,
          "en": "Strings, numbers, Booleans are all primitives.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 23.1,
          "end": 26.19,
          "en": "What's special about primitives in JavaScript",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 26.19,
          "end": 28.68,
          "en": "is that we can't edit them.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 28.68,
          "end": 31.35,
          "en": "I can, of course, overwrite user message",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 31.35,
          "end": 35.37,
          "en": "and store \"Hello\" there, but that's a brand new string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 35.37,
          "end": 38.04,
          "en": "The old string, which was stored in memory",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 38.04,
          "end": 39.78,
          "en": "will simply be thrown away.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 39.78,
          "end": 42.483,
          "en": "It's not edited to be this string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 43.65,
          "end": 48.09,
          "en": "Even if I execute a method on user message,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 48.09,
          "end": 52.44,
          "en": "because just like arrays, strings do come with methods,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 52.44,
          "end": 55.23,
          "en": "you can call on them and I, for example,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 55.23,
          "end": 59.37,
          "en": "concat another string by using the concat method,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 59.37,
          "end": 61.8,
          "en": "this will produce a new string",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 61.8,
          "end": 64.77,
          "en": "instead of editing the old string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 64.77,
          "end": 68.88,
          "en": "So when dealing with strings and numbers and Booleans,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 68.88,
          "end": 71.7,
          "en": "you always produce new values.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 71.7,
          "end": 74.103,
          "en": "You can't edit existing values.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 75.18,
          "end": 78.33,
          "en": "This changes when you're dealing with objects,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 78.33,
          "end": 81.99,
          "en": "objects and arrays, which technically as mentioned before",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 81.99,
          "end": 83.7,
          "en": "are objects.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 83.7,
          "end": 86.01,
          "en": "If I have a hobbies array here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 86.01,
          "end": 88.92,
          "en": "with sports and cooking let's say,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 88.92,
          "end": 92.07,
          "en": "and I then use, for example, the push method,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 92.07,
          "end": 93.78,
          "en": "which is a built-in method",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 93.78,
          "end": 96.15,
          "en": "to also add \"Working\" to that array",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 96.15,
          "end": 99.45,
          "en": "I edited the original array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 99.45,
          "end": 103.83,
          "en": "If I console log hobbies here, I get this new array",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 103.83,
          "end": 106.38300000000001,
          "en": "which contains working as a third item.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 107.58,
          "end": 111.33,
          "en": "Because push indeed edited the original array,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 111.33,
          "end": 115.02000000000001,
          "en": "it mutated the original array.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 115.02000000000001,
          "end": 117.21000000000001,
          "en": "Because arrays are objects",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 117.21000000000001,
          "end": 122.21,
          "en": "and objects in JavaScript are so-called reference values,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 122.91,
          "end": 126.33,
          "en": "which in the end, to simplify it a bit",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 126.33,
          "end": 131.22,
          "en": "simply means that in a variable you don't store the value,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 131.22,
          "end": 136.22,
          "en": "but instead the address of that value in memory.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 136.23,
          "end": 139.8,
          "en": "So that array is stored somewhere in memory,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 139.8,
          "end": 142.05,
          "en": "so in the memory of your computer,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 142.05,
          "end": 144.93,
          "en": "and it's the address of that place in memory",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 144.93,
          "end": 146.823,
          "en": "that's stored in this constant.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 147.93,
          "end": 150.6,
          "en": "When you then call push, for example,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 150.6,
          "end": 154.98,
          "en": "JavaScript will reach out to that address,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 154.98,
          "end": 157.56,
          "en": "open the value in that address,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 157.56,
          "end": 160.68,
          "en": "so take a look at that array in this case,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 160.68,
          "end": 164.793,
          "en": "and add this new item to this existing array in memory.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 165.63,
          "end": 169.59300000000002,
          "en": "So the array in memory changes, the address does not.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 170.97,
          "end": 175.97,
          "en": "With primitive values like this string here,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 176.16,
          "end": 178.71,
          "en": "you could instead say that the string itself",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 178.71,
          "end": 181.2,
          "en": "is stored in the variable.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 181.2,
          "end": 183.87,
          "en": "This is technically not 100% correct,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 183.87,
          "end": 185.97,
          "en": "but you can think of it like this.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 185.97,
          "end": 189.27,
          "en": "So you don't have this address thing here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 189.27,
          "end": 192.9,
          "en": "This instead only matters when you're dealing with objects.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 192.9,
          "end": 195.693,
          "en": "There, it's this address that's being stored,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 60,
          "start": 196.56,
          "end": 200.25,
          "en": "and that's all the reason why I can edit this array",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 61,
          "start": 200.25,
          "end": 202.89,
          "en": "despite using const here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 62,
          "start": 202.89,
          "end": 206.67000000000002,
          "en": "Const means that the value can't be edited, right?",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 63,
          "start": 206.67000000000002,
          "end": 209.1,
          "en": "And still I can do it here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 64,
          "start": 209.1,
          "end": 212.1,
          "en": "Well, because const technically does not mean",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 65,
          "start": 212.1,
          "end": 214.17000000000002,
          "en": "that the value can't be edited,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 66,
          "start": 214.17000000000002,
          "end": 216.78300000000002,
          "en": "but that the variable can't be overwritten.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 67,
          "start": 217.8,
          "end": 218.91,
          "en": "So when using const,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 68,
          "start": 218.91,
          "end": 223.2,
          "en": "I can't use the equal sign operator to assign a new value.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 69,
          "start": 223.2,
          "end": 224.88,
          "en": "That won't work.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 70,
          "start": 224.88,
          "end": 227.163,
          "en": "If I save this, I'm getting an error.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 71,
          "start": 228.99,
          "end": 233.88,
          "en": "But I can absolutely store objects in a constant",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 72,
          "start": 233.88,
          "end": 235.98,
          "en": "and then take advantage of the fact",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 73,
          "start": 235.98,
          "end": 240.98,
          "en": "that those objects are accessed by reference, by address",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 74,
          "start": 241.77,
          "end": 246.123,
          "en": "to manipulate those values in memory by using that address.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 75,
          "start": 247.23,
          "end": 249.21,
          "en": "And I'm just mentioning this here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 76,
          "start": 249.21,
          "end": 251.85,
          "en": "because it can sometimes be confusing",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 77,
          "start": 251.85,
          "end": 255.75,
          "en": "that I can edit an array, for example, with push",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 78,
          "start": 255.75,
          "end": 259.92,
          "en": "despite that array being stored in a constant.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 79,
          "start": 259.92,
          "end": 262.5,
          "en": "Well, as I just explained, I can do that",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 80,
          "start": 262.5,
          "end": 265.74,
          "en": "because I'm storing the address in this constant",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 81,
          "start": 265.74,
          "end": 268.26,
          "en": "and the address doesn't change,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 82,
          "start": 268.26,
          "end": 270.54,
          "en": "so I'm not violating this idea",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 83,
          "start": 270.54,
          "end": 274.95,
          "en": "of having a constant variable, which is not overwritten,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 84,
          "start": 274.95,
          "end": 277.23,
          "en": "but I'm instead editing the value",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 85,
          "start": 277.23,
          "end": 279.63,
          "en": "that is behind that address.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 86,
          "start": 279.63,
          "end": 282.903,
          "en": "And that in turn, absolutely is allowed.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "errors": []
}